.TH "UNLOCK" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
unlock \- decrypt a file made by lock, age or gpg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
unlock [options] file ... [-- age or gpg options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBunlock\fR writes the plain copy of an encrypted file next to it, and keeps the encrypted file. \fBsecrets.env.age\fR gives \fBsecrets.env\fR. A name with \fB.tar\fR before the ending holds a folder, so \fBphotos.tar.age\fR gives the folder \fBphotos/\fR.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
secrets.env.age -> secrets.env (1.3KB, 1.9s)
.fi
.RE
.PP
It opens files from \fBlock\fR, and any other file made with \fBage\fR or \fBgpg\fR. It tells the two apart from the first bytes of the file, not from the name, so a gpg file called \fBnotes.age\fR still opens with gpg.
.TP
\fBnotes.txt.age\fR, \fB.gpg\fR, \fB.asc\fR or \fB.pgp\fR
\fBnotes.txt\fR
.TP
\fBphotos.tar.age\fR or \fBphotos.tar.gpg\fR
the folder \fBphotos/\fR
.TP
anything else
nothing until you name the output with \fB\-o\fR, or print it with \fB\-\-cat\fR
.PP
When the file needs a passphrase, age or gpg asks for it. When it is locked to a key, \fBunlock\fR finds the key:
.IP \(bu 2
For an age file, it tries \fB~/.config/age/keys.txt\fR, \fB~/.ssh/id_ed25519\fR and \fB~/.ssh/id_rsa\fR, in that order, and says which ones it used. \fB\-i\fR names a key in another place.
.IP \(bu 2
For a gpg file, gpg looks in your keyring as always.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity FILE\fR
The private key for an age file. Can repeat. Without it \fBunlock\fR tries the usual places above.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out NAME\fR
Name the output yourself. Only with one file. For a folder, it names the folder.
.TP
\fB\-\-cat\fR
Print the plain content to stdout and write nothing.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Show only the result line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show every step, and each real command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunlock\fR never overwrites. When the output name is taken, it stops with exit 4 and names \fB\-o\fR and \fB\-\-cat\fR as the ways out.
.IP \(bu 2
It decrypts into a hidden temp file or folder beside the output, and renames it only when age or gpg finished without an error. A wrong passphrase, a wrong key or a damaged file leaves nothing behind.
.IP \(bu 2
A folder comes out of a temp folder in one rename, so you never see half a folder.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-cat\fR on a folder, with the output going to a terminal, stops with exit 2. A tar stream on screen is noise. Pipe it to \fBtar\fR instead.
.PP
The plain copy lands on disk. For a secret you only need to read once, \fB\-\-cat\fR keeps it off the disk.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to \fBage \-d\fR for age files and to \fBgpg \-\-decrypt\fR for gpg files:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock report.pdf.gpg -- --pinentry-mode loopback
$ unlock backup.tar.gpg -- --ignore-mdc-error
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-ignore\-mdc\-error\fR opens very old gpg files that have no integrity check. Use it only on files you trust.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBage\fR for age files and \fBgpg\fR for gpg files. You only need the one your files use. \fBtar\fR for folders.
.TP
Debian, Ubuntu
age: \fBsudo apt install age\fR. gpg: \fBsudo apt install gnupg\fR
.TP
Fedora
age: \fBsudo dnf install age\fR. gpg: \fBsudo dnf install gnupg2\fR
.TP
Arch
age: \fBsudo pacman \-S age\fR. gpg: \fBsudo pacman \-S gnupg\fR
.TP
openSUSE
age: \fBsudo zypper install age\fR. gpg: \fBsudo zypper install gpg2\fR
.TP
Alpine
age: \fBsudo apk add age\fR. gpg: \fBsudo apk add gnupg\fR
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A file with a passphrase
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock secrets.env.age
Enter passphrase:
secrets.env.age -> secrets.env (1.3KB, 1.9s)
.fi
.RE
.SS A folder
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock photos.tar.gpg
photos.tar.gpg -> photos/ (8.7KB, 3 files, 0.1s)
.fi
.RE
.SS Locked to your SSH key
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock photos.tar.age
unlock: using ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
photos.tar.age -> photos/ (8.9KB, 3 files, 0.0s)
.fi
.RE
.SS A key somewhere else
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock -i ~/keys/work.txt report.pdf.age
report.pdf.age -> report.pdf (212KB, 0.0s)
.fi
.RE
.SS Read one line and write nothing
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock --cat secrets.env.age | grep DB_HOST
Enter passphrase:
DB_HOST=db.example.com
.fi
.RE
.SS See what is in a locked folder
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock --cat photos.tar.age | tar -tv
drwxr-xr-x me/me             0 2026-09-29 16:40 photos/
-rw-r--r-- me/me          4201 2026-09-29 16:40 photos/IMG_4410.jpg
-rw-r--r-- me/me          3890 2026-09-29 16:40 photos/IMG_4411.jpg
-rw-r--r-- me/me            14 2026-09-29 16:40 photos/albums.txt
.fi
.RE
.SS A gpg file, step by step
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock -v secrets.env.gpg
+ gpg --no-symkey-cache --decrypt -o - -- secrets.env.gpg > secrets.env
gpg: AES256.CFB encrypted data
gpg: encrypted with 1 passphrase
secrets.env.gpg -> secrets.env (1.3KB, 0.1s)
.fi
.RE
.SS The plain copy is already there
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock secrets.env.gpg
unlock: secrets.env exists. Use -o for another name, or --cat
$ unlock -o secrets.new secrets.env.gpg
secrets.env.gpg -> secrets.new (1.3KB, 0.1s)
.fi
.RE
.SS A wrong passphrase
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ unlock secrets.env.gpg
gpg: decryption failed: Bad session key
unlock: could not decrypt secrets.env.gpg, wrong passphrase or key. Nothing was written
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBunlock: X is locked to a key, and there is no key in ~/.config/age/keys.txt or ~/.ssh. Name it with \-i\fR : The age file needs a private key that is not in the usual places. Pass it with \fB\-i\fR.
.PP
\fBage: error: no identity matched any of the recipients\fR : None of your keys can open it. It was locked for someone else, or for a key you no longer have.
.PP
\fBunlock: X is not an age or gpg file\fR : The file does not start like either. It may be damaged, or made by another tool such as \fBopenssl\fR or \fBzip \-e\fR.
.PP
\fBgpg: problem with the agent: Inappropriate ioctl for device\fR : gpg cannot find the terminal. Run \fBexport GPG_TTY=$(tty)\fR and try again.
.PP
\fBunlock\fR did not ask for the gpg passphrase : gpg\-agent remembered it from an earlier run. \fBlock\fR and \fBunlock\fR turn that off with \fB\-\-no\-symkey\-cache\fR, but plain gpg does not. \fBgpgconf \-\-reload gpg\-agent\fR makes it forget.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Every file was unlocked.
.TP
1
It failed. A wrong passphrase or key, no key found, or a file that is not age or gpg. Nothing was written for it.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as a file with no known ending and no \fB\-o\fR.
.TP
3
age or gpg is missing, whichever the file needs.
.TP
4
Refused. The output name is taken.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBlock\fR, \fBage(1)\fR, \fBgpg(1)\fR, \fBtar(1)\fR
